\title[Restricted sumsets along many lines in $\FF_p^2$]{Restricted Sumsets Along Many Lines in $\FF_p^2$:\\ A Sharp Triple Criterion and the Failure of the $(k-2)(p-1)$ Threshold}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $p$ be a prime, let $v_1,\dots,v_k$ be pairwise linearly independent directions in the plane $\FF_p^2$, and let $S_1,\dots,S_k \subseteq \FF_p$ be step-sets each containing $0$. The \emph{reach} of the configuration is the restricted sumset $\Reach(v,S) = \{\sum_i s_i v_i : s_i \in S_i\}$, and the \emph{total deficiency} is $D(S)=\sum_i (p-|S_i|)$. A natural conjecture --- suggested both by the degree count in Alon's Combinatorial Nullstellensatz and by iterated Cauchy--Davenport --- asserts that $D(S) \le (k-2)(p-1)$ forces $\Reach(v,S)=\FF_p^2$. We settle it. The conjecture holds for $k=3$, in the sharp form $D(S)<p$, and we give a short covering proof. It fails for every $k\ge 4$ and every prime $p\ge 3$: the \emph{harmonic quadruple} $(1,0),(0,1),(1,1),(-1,1)$ with step-sets $\FF_p\setminus\{1\},\FF_p\setminus\{1\},\{0,1\},\{0,1\}$, padded by trivial sets $\{0\}$, attains total deficiency exactly $(k-2)(p-1)$ and misses the point $(1,2)$, throughout the entire feasible range $4\le k\le p+1$. In place of the false conjecture we prove a criterion valid for all $k$ and optimal: if some three distinct indices satisfy $d_i+d_j+d_l<p$, where $d_i=p-|S_i|$, then the reach is everything; the counterexample family shows the strict inequality cannot be relaxed to $\le p$. We also delimit exactly what the conjectured threshold does buy: under $D(S)\le (k-2)(p-1)$ the reach surjects onto every quotient line, hence misses no whole line, and if some $S_{i_0}=\FF_p$ the conjectured bound is valid after all. Finally we give the polynomial-method reformulation: $(k-2)(p-1)$ is precisely the degree budget making an admissible monomial of $L_1^{p-1}L_2^{p-1}$ available, so the conjecture can only fail through simultaneous vanishing of all admissible coefficients, which is exactly what occurs in the harmonic family.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{restricted sumsets, Cauchy--Davenport, Combinatorial Nullstellensatz, finite plane, harmonic quadruple, polynomial method}

